# Structurizr Lite staging (built by `make c4-render`)
.structurizr/
.claude/settings.local.json
# Rust codegen build artifacts (ADR-0034)
tools/codegen-rs/target/
target/
# Ephemeral codegen scratch (e.g. `make c4-export` .mmd output)
tools/codegen-rs/out/

# Agent worktrees (temporary, auto-managed)
.claude/worktrees/

# Agent worktrees created inside the repo. Sessions have repeatedly made these as
# `wt2/`, `wt-main/`, `wt7/` ... An in-repo worktree shows up as untracked, and a
# `git add -A` would commit an entire second checkout into the tree. Ignoring them
# makes that mistake unspellable rather than merely discouraged. Prefer /tmp for a
# worktree; this is the safety net for when someone does not.
wt*/

# Register-check hook firing log (runtime artifact, ADR-20260821-010543)
.claude/register-check.log

# QMD advisory retrieval cache — derived, disposable, never authoritative (row RETRIEVAL-QMD-CI)
.qmd/

# Python bytecode caches — a .pyc with no source in this tree was committed once by an over-broad
# `git add` (review #8 of PR #679). Nothing in-tree runs a .py FILE today: every call site passes
# the program on the COMMAND LINE (`python3 -c`) or on STDIN (`python3 - "$1" <<'PYEOF'`, twice in
# `register-check-selftest.sh`), and the interpreter writes no bytecode for either. The
# `PYTHONPATH=` probes in `stub-tests.sh` DO write `__pycache__/sitecustomize.*.pyc` -- but into
# their `mktemp -d` fixture, which the EXIT trap removes, so nothing reaches this tree. An earlier
# version of this sentence said "both Python call sites here use `python3 -c`": there are ~20 across
# the gate scripts, two of them stdin heredocs, and the `PYTHONPATH` six do write bytecode -- the
# conclusion held and every stated reason for it was wrong (review #83). So this is insurance, not a
# fix for an observed dirtying — the first version of this comment invented the cause (`running
# .github/scripts/*.py`, a directory that does not exist on this branch), which is the defect this
# PR catalogues nine times, in the comment justifying the entry.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
